uction","scope_step_ids":["contradict-lowest-terms"],"statement":"sqrt(2) is not rational.","step_ids":["contradict-lowest-terms"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"define-lowest-terms","justification":"If both integers were divisible by 2, then their fraction could be reduced by cancelling 2; thus a lowest-terms fraction cannot have both entries even.","statement":"Record the needed meaning of “in lowest terms”: a and b are not both even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"assume-representation","justification":"This is the temporary negation of the desired irrationality conclusion.","statement":"Assume sqrt(2)=a/b for integers a,b, b nonzero, with a/b in lowest terms."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["assume-representation"],"id":"derive-squared-equation","justification":"Squaring gives 2=a^2/b^2; multiplying by b^2 gives a^2=2b^2.","statement":"Derive a^2=2b^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["derive-squared-equation"],"id":"prove-a-even","justification":"The right side is even. If a were odd, a=2k+1 for some integer k, so a^2=4k(k+1)+1, which is odd. This contradicts a^2=2b^2. Hence a is even.","statement":"Prove that a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["derive-squared-equation","prove-a-even"],"id":"substitute-a-even","justification":"Write a=2c. Then 4c^2=2b^2, and division by 2 gives b^2=2c^2, so b^2 is even.","statement":"Show that b^2 is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["substitute-a-even"],"id":"prove-b-even","justification":"If b were odd, the calculation in prove-a-even, with b in place of a, would make b^2 odd, contrary to b^2=2c^2. Hence b is even.","statement":"Prove that b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["define-lowest-terms","assume-representation","prove-a-even","prove-b-even"],"id":"contradict-lowest-terms","justification":"The assumption says a/b is in lowest terms, while prove-a-even and prove-b-even say both entries are divisible by 2. This contradiction discharges 